SEM={
'家珍':('one’s own household treasure',['one’s own treasure','the treasure at home','native treasure'],'One’s own household treasure is what belongs to oneself rather than something brought in through the gate or counted in another person’s store.','The records turn domestic wealth into a test of direct possession: masters contrast what enters from outside with what comes from one’s own breast, ask hearers to recognize it, and call the whole field of sights and sounds their own treasure. The image does not name money or a hidden jewel; it challenges reliance on borrowed words.','Literal donated or discarded household property is not automatically this evaluative use; the selected cases explicitly oppose own treasure to imported or counted treasure.'),
'續燈錄':('a continuation of the lamp record',['continued lamp record','supplementary lamp history','Continuation of the Lamp Record'],'A continuation of the lamp record is a later lineage compilation that extends an earlier transmission history with further masters, cases, and succession claims.','The corpus uses the title both for the Song compilation submitted by Weibo and for later projects that gather additional masters. Chan authors also cite such records as evidence in disputes over who belongs to whose line, so the book title participates in lineage adjudication rather than merely naming a volume.','The phrase is a bibliographic title or title class. Incidental adjacency of 續 and 燈錄 across headings is excluded, and different compilations do not become different lexical senses.'),
'鐵牛之機':('the mechanism of the iron ox',['iron-ox mechanism','iron-ox working','the iron ox’s mechanism'],'The mechanism of the iron ox is an image for a working that cannot be caught, shifted, or stamped by ordinary handling.','Fengxue’s inherited formulation says the ancestral seal resembles it: going is stamped fast, staying breaks the stamp, and neither going nor staying leaves the examiner without a demand. Yuanwu calls it impossible to cage; later masters quote the case to test whether a claimant can actually deploy such a mechanism. The iron ox supplies immovability, while the final word makes that apparent fixity an active testing function.','The phrase is not evidence for a historical iron machine, and the shorter iron-ox expression may have other literal or figurative deployments.'),
'監寺':('the monastery supervisor',['monastery administrator','supervising steward','temple supervisor'],'The monastery supervisor is a named administrative officer responsible for communal property, personnel, and practical monastery business.','Rules and records place the supervisor among the eastern administrative offices, invite or thank the officeholder in public ceremonies, and show individual supervisors commissioning addresses or being named in verses. The office is institutional: it does not make its holder the presiding teacher.','Personal names followed by 監寺 identify holders of the same office; catalogue headings and invitation labels are documentary evidence, not speech by the officeholder.'),
'陶淵明':('Tao Yuanming, the poet who returned home',['Tao Qian','Master Five Willows','the poet of Return'],'Tao Yuanming is the poet repeatedly invoked as the person who drinks, values chrysanthemums, and turns away to return home.','Chan masters make his recognizable refusal do work in public speech: “if it were Tao Yuanming, he would frown and go home” dismisses over-elaboration, while references to wine, chrysanthemums, and the Return Home rhapsody supply a compact figure for withdrawal. He is a pre-Chan literary figure as deployed by Chan records, not a lineage master.','The entry describes the corpus’s Tao figure; it does not claim every mention of wine, chrysanthemums, or returning home alludes to him.'),}
